 "eras": [
  {"id":"1609","year":"1609","label":"Ikeda Terumasa's keep complex","short":"1609","lens":"none","summary":"Ikeda Terumasa has rebuilt Himeji since 1601; the Daitenshu is completed in 1609, with the gates, yagura and walls of the inner castle.","basis":"The OSM-mapped keep outline, named gates and yagura, wall lines and the inner moat remains, carried back as inferred because the Agency for Cultural Affairs text says most surviving buildings and the layout date from Ikeda's time. Honda's West Bailey buildings and the Kesho-yagura are left out.","conjecture":"No 1609 plan was found. Hideyoshi's three-storey keep of 1581 (about 55 m2 floor) was removed by Ikeda and its position is not documented. Any earlier Nishi-no-maru enclosure is unmapped. The middle and outer moats (the outer is now buried) and the castle town are not in the window or not mapped. Which walls and gates are 1609 and which later repair is not known.","sources":["HIM-01","HIM-02","HIM-03","HIM-04","HIM-08"],"facts":["Ikeda began work in 1601 and the Dai-Tenshu was completed in 1609 (Hyogo chronology).","Ikeda removed Hideyoshi's 1581 three-storey keep (Wikipedia).","The keep is six floors over a basement: 31.5 m on a 14.85 m stone base, 46.4 m in all (tabi-mag.jp; Wikipedia).","The Ro no watariyagura is dated around 1609 (Heritage Database)."]},
  {"id":"1618","year":"1617-18","label":"Honda Tadamasa's West Bailey","short":"1618","lens":"none","summary":"Honda Tadamasa, lord from 1617, develops the Nishi-no-maru (West Bailey) with earthen walls, watchtowers and a long gallery, and adds the Kesho-yagura for Princess Sen.","basis":"The 1609 set plus the OSM-mapped West Bailey yagura and watariyagura (read from the raw OSM dump), the Kesho-yagura and the wall lines inside the West Bailey, all carried back as inferred from the present map.","conjecture":"No plan of 1618 was found. The long gallery (about 240 m on himejicastle.jp) is not drawn between the mapped towers: its route is not documented. The Meiji-era palace in the Nishi-no-maru, later military buildings and later repairs are not drawn.","sources":["HIM-01","HIM-02","HIM-03","HIM-04"],"facts":["Honda Tadamasa became lord in 1617; Nishi no Maru was improved in 1616-17 as a residence for Princess Sen and Honda Tadatoki (Hyogo chronology).","The West Bailey has earthen walls, watchtowers and a long gallery with watchtowers at each end (himejicastle.jp)."]},
  {"id":"today","year":"Today","label":"World Heritage site","short":"Present","lens":"none","summary":"A World Heritage site since 1993 and a Special Historic Site within the middle moat; the Daitenshu was repaired 1956-64 and again 2010-15.","basis":"Everything drawn is OpenStreetMap (ODbL) and the Terrarium tiles, with the keep height from the OSM tag. Modern visitor buildings, paths and the zoo to the east are mapped as they are.","conjecture":"Walls and buildings have no sourced height and are kerb-high plan lines, except the keep outline's tagged 46.4 m. The DEM (about 30 m source) reads 54-60 m at the keep, above the published hilltop of 45.6 m, and cannot show the terraced stone walls (up to 26 m high). The three small keeps and the Nishi-no-maru palace footprint are not mapped.","sources":["HIM-02","HIM-05","HIM-06"],"facts":["The castle survived the 1945 air raid and the 1995 earthquake (Wikipedia).","Wikipedia counts 83 structures, of which 74 Important Cultural Properties; 21 of the original 84 gates remain.","Three moats originally; the outer is now buried (Wikipedia)."]}
 ],
